    "sixtySeconds": {
      "whatItDoes": "Stagehand is an SDK for AI browser automation. You write normal Playwright-style code (goto, click, locator) and add natural-language steps: observe finds elements and returns real selectors, act performs actions and self-heals when a site is redesigned, and extract returns schema-validated data. It runs locally with Chrome or on Browserbase, and there's a browse CLI and skill for coding agents.",
      "problem": "Browser scripts break when sites change, and fully autonomous agents are unpredictable. Stagehand lets you choose code or AI for each step.",
      "whoFor": [
        "Developers automating websites",
        "Teams building browser agents",
        "Coding-agent users who need a browser"
      ],
      "whyChoose": "Choose it for familiar APIs, self-healing actions, typed data extraction and SDKs for TypeScript, Python and Go.",
      "requirements": [
        "Chrome for local runs",
        "Node.js, Python or Go",
        "A model API key for AI steps (not needed for the local browse CLI)"
      ]
    },
    "overview": [
      "Install the SDK for your language. Local runs need Chrome; the full setup is in the Quickstart docs.",
      "A session can persist cookies in a user data folder so you stay signed in. observe returns selectors, so credentials you fill in never reach the model.",
      "Coding agents can use the browse CLI and skill instead, with no LLM key or Browserbase account needed locally. Browserbase hosting and a hosted MCP are optional. It is MIT licensed."
    ],
